  "body": "`site/content/guide/operations.md` covers every operation kind in about 800 words. Give each kind its own reference page (or one section per kind on a page per group), so a user can look up the operation they're about to use.\n\nEach page or section should give:\n- what the operation is for and when to choose it over a neighbour (for example, adaptive vs pocket);\n- the parameters that matter most, by tab, with their units and sensible starting values;\n- the geometry it takes (which picks: faces, edges, chains, holes) and what a click picks;\n- a picture of a typical toolpath;\n- the refusals a user is most likely to hit, and the fix for each (link the troubleshooting page, #1244).\n\nGroups, one page each (or one page with sections):\n- 2D: facing, contour, pocket, slot, trochoidal, chamfer, engrave, trace, deburr\n- 3D: adaptive, parallel, scallop, pencil, contour, and the rest of the 3D finishing set\n- Holes: drill cycles, bore, thread milling, tap\n- Multi-axis: rotary, swarf, flow, multi-axis contour and roughing\n- Turning: facing, profiling, grooving, threading, boring, live tooling\n- Probing\n- Plasma\n- EDM: wire and sinker (once those ship)\n\nTake the exact list of kinds from `OpType` on main when the work starts. Each parameter description comes from CamCraft's own behaviour and general machining knowledge, never from another product's help text (Clean-Room Protocol).\n\nDone means: every user-creatable operation kind has its own page or section in the guide, with what it's for, its key parameters, a toolpath picture and its common refusals.\n",
